  "response": "Here is a Python function named `business_days(date1, date2)` that calculates the number of business days (Monday through Friday) between two given dates. Both dates must be in the format `\"YYYY-MM-DD\"`, and neither date is included in the count. Public holidays are not considered.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\n\ndef business_days(date1: str, date2: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the number of business days (Monday-Friday) between two dates.\n    \n    Parameters:\n        date1 (str): First date in \"YYYY-MM-DD\" format.\n        date2 (str): Second date in \"YYYY-MM-DD\" format.\n        \n    Returns:\n        int: Number of business days strictly between the two dates (exclusive).\n    \"\"\"\n    d1 = date.fromisoformat(date1)\n    d2 = date.fromisoformat(date2)\n    \n    if d1 == d2:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Ensure date1 is before date2\n    start, end = sorted([d1, d2])\n    \n    count = 0\n    current_date = start + timedelta(days=1)\n    \n    while current_date < end:\n        if current_date.weekday() < 5:  # 0=Monday through 4=Friday\n            count += 1\n        current_date += timedelta(days=1)\n    \n    return count\n```\n\n### Example Usage:\n```python\nprint(business_days(\"2024-01-01\", \"2024-01-08\"))  # Output: 4\nprint(business_days(\"2024-01-05\", \"2024-01-06\"))  # Output: 0\nprint(business_days(\"2024-01-07\", \"2024-01-08\"))  # Output: 0\nprint(business_days(\"2024-01-07\", \"2024-01-05\"))  # Output: 3 (order doesn't matter)\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- Only weekdays (Monday–Friday) are counted.\n- The start and end dates themselves are excluded from the count.\n- No public holidays or special rules for weekends/holidays are included.\n- Works correctly regardless of date order.",
